1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,400
He called Trump's comeback.

2
00:00:01,870 --> 00:00:04,340
He called a war between America and Iran.

3
00:00:04,710 --> 00:00:07,180
Now he says civilization is collapsing...

4
00:00:07,470 --> 00:00:09,370
and that one day, the money in your bank

5
00:00:09,370 --> 00:00:10,680
account could just vanish.

6
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:12,890
Millions of people are listening to him.

7
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:15,270
So we checked everything he said about money.

8
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:17,000
Claim by claim.

9
00:00:17,330 --> 00:00:19,480
And stay to the end, because he made a

10
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:21,580
bet about Trump... and we looked up what the

11
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:23,310
Constitution actually says.

12
00:00:23,730 --> 00:00:25,360
This is The Arab Ledger.

13
00:00:25,770 --> 00:00:27,440
I'm Tall. Let's go.

14
00:00:28,490 --> 00:00:30,680
His name is Jiang Xueqin.

15
00:00:31,550 --> 00:00:34,000
Online, people call him Professor Jiang.

16
00:00:34,270 --> 00:00:36,340
Some call him the Nostradamus of China.

17
00:00:36,710 --> 00:00:38,990
He was born in China, grew up in Canada,

18
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:40,210
and studied at Yale.

19
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:43,180
For years he ran high school programs in Shenzhen

20
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:43,810
and Beijing.

21
00:00:44,130 --> 00:00:46,690
His channel has nearly three million subscribers.

22
00:00:47,110 --> 00:00:50,160
In twenty twenty-four, he predicted Trump would win, and

23
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:52,110
that America would end up at war with Iran.

24
00:00:52,570 --> 00:00:53,300
Both happened.

25
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:55,960
He also predicted America would lose that war.

26
00:00:56,490 --> 00:00:57,890
That one is still open.

27
00:00:58,290 --> 00:01:01,370
Critics say many analysts saw the same risks.

28
00:01:01,650 --> 00:01:03,380
He just said it earlier, and louder.

29
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:06,060
Either way, when he talks, people listen.

30
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:07,400
So let's listen.

31
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:10,520
Here's his theory, the way he tells it.

32
00:01:10,830 --> 00:01:13,800
After World War Two, America became the center of

33
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:14,570
the world's money.

34
00:01:14,950 --> 00:01:18,270
In nineteen seventy-one, President Nixon cut the dollar's link

35
00:01:18,270 --> 00:01:20,860
to gold. So America needed a new reason for

36
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:22,120
the world to want dollars.

37
00:01:24,350 --> 00:01:26,290
His answer: oil. Saudi Arabia agreed to sell its

38
00:01:26,290 --> 00:01:30,440
oil in dollars. Then, he says, China demanded dollars

39
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:32,850
for its goods, and Japan agreed to pour its

40
00:01:32,850 --> 00:01:34,200
savings into American debt.

41
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:37,260
That system, he says, built the modern world.

42
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:40,220
Every phone and every chip needs the whole planet

43
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:40,970
trading together.

44
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:42,580
He even gives a formula.

45
00:01:43,030 --> 00:01:46,940
Progress equals specialization, times globalization.

46
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:50,420
And now, he says, the war with Iran is

47
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:51,520
breaking that chain.

48
00:01:52,050 --> 00:01:55,050
Oil stops flowing. Asia stops making things.

49
00:01:55,350 --> 00:01:57,290
The dollars stop coming back to America.

50
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:00,300
And, in his words, maybe in a few months...

51
00:02:00,570 --> 00:02:01,670
the system collapses.

52
00:02:02,070 --> 00:02:04,220
Before we check it: which part do you think

53
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:06,660
is true? Put your guess in the comments.

54
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:10,400
Seven claims. Seven verdicts.

55
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:14,530
One. Nixon cut the dollar from gold, and oil

56
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:15,600
got priced in dollars.

57
00:02:16,120 --> 00:02:19,500
In nineteen seventy-one, Nixon ended the dollar's gold link.

58
00:02:20,010 --> 00:02:22,230
Oil was already priced in dollars before that.

59
00:02:22,670 --> 00:02:25,740
In nineteen seventy-four, America and Saudi Arabia made a

60
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:28,800
secret deal: Saudi oil money went into American debt.

61
00:02:29,110 --> 00:02:31,290
There was never a signed promise to sell oil

62
00:02:31,290 --> 00:02:33,660
only in dollars. But the deal helped keep it

63
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:34,260
that way.

64
00:02:34,530 --> 00:02:35,790
Verdict: mostly true.

65
00:02:36,890 --> 00:02:40,250
Two. China demanded that the world pay in dollars.

66
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:44,400
In nineteen eighty, China's exports were tiny, about eighteen

67
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:45,550
billion dollars a year.

68
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:47,550
It wasn't setting the rules.

69
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:49,940
It used the dollar because everyone did.

70
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:53,170
Later, China did pile up dollars and buy American

71
00:02:53,170 --> 00:02:55,750
debt. But it never made the world pay in

72
00:02:55,750 --> 00:02:56,350
dollars.

73
00:02:56,640 --> 00:02:59,200
Verdict: false. China followed the dollar.

74
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:00,130
It didn't create it.

75
00:03:01,150 --> 00:03:04,200
Three. Japan agreed to put all its savings into

76
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:07,460
American debt. He calls this the Plaza Accord.

77
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:09,490
The Plaza Accord was real.

78
00:03:09,970 --> 00:03:13,940
Nineteen eighty-five, five big economies, one goal: push the

79
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:16,450
dollar down against the yen and the German mark.

80
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:18,730
It was a deal about exchange rates.

81
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:21,140
Not a promise to buy American debt.

82
00:03:21,490 --> 00:03:23,930
Japan did buy plenty of American debt in the

83
00:03:23,930 --> 00:03:26,680
eighties. But the Plaza deal didn't require it.

84
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:28,250
Verdict: false.

85
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:32,850
Four. Japan, the Gulf and China are America's three

86
00:03:32,850 --> 00:03:33,680
biggest lenders.

87
00:03:34,120 --> 00:03:36,070
We checked the latest US Treasury data.

88
00:03:36,650 --> 00:03:39,640
Japan is first, with just over one trillion dollars.

89
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:41,860
But number two... is Britain.

90
00:03:42,350 --> 00:03:44,810
It just passed one trillion for the first time.

91
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:47,910
China is third, and its holdings are the lowest

92
00:03:47,910 --> 00:03:48,890
since two thousand eight.

93
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:52,810
And the Gulf? Its two biggest holders, Saudi Arabia

94
00:03:52,810 --> 00:03:55,770
and the Emirates, together hold about two hundred and

95
00:03:55,770 --> 00:03:58,000
fifty billion. Less than three percent.

96
00:03:58,330 --> 00:04:01,100
Gulf money in America is much bigger in stocks

97
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:04,100
and funds. But as lenders to the US government,

98
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:04,890
not close.

99
00:04:05,310 --> 00:04:08,440
Verdict: half true. Japan and China, yes.

100
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:09,720
The Gulf, not even close.

101
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:14,310
Five. China dominates the minerals inside chips.

102
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:16,970
He said China makes sixty to seventy percent of

103
00:04:16,970 --> 00:04:20,180
the world's silicon, seventy percent of gallium, and ninety

104
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:21,000
percent of germanium.

105
00:04:21,610 --> 00:04:24,410
The real numbers: almost eighty percent of silicon.

106
00:04:24,770 --> 00:04:26,610
Around ninety-nine percent of gallium.

107
00:04:26,610 --> 00:04:29,520
And germanium, about ninety percent, just like he said.

108
00:04:29,910 --> 00:04:32,710
Verdict: true. If anything, he understated it.

109
00:04:33,770 --> 00:04:37,880
Six. One small American town controls the world's purest

110
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:38,480
quartz.

111
00:04:38,890 --> 00:04:40,380
Spruce Pine, North Carolina.

112
00:04:40,810 --> 00:04:42,540
Population: about two thousand.

113
00:04:42,950 --> 00:04:45,750
Its mines supply roughly seventy to ninety percent of

114
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:48,500
the world's high-purity quartz, used in making chips.

115
00:04:49,290 --> 00:04:52,150
Verdict: true. And it shows supply chains run both

116
00:04:52,150 --> 00:04:52,750
ways.

117
00:04:53,650 --> 00:04:56,680
Seven. The heart of it: maybe within months, the

118
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:57,700
system collapses.

119
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:00,330
If the world were running from the dollar, foreigners

120
00:05:00,330 --> 00:05:01,810
would be dumping American debt.

121
00:05:02,270 --> 00:05:05,270
Instead, foreign holdings went up in August.

122
00:05:05,630 --> 00:05:07,860
About nine point three trillion dollars.

123
00:05:08,230 --> 00:05:10,660
Close to the all-time high set in February.

124
00:05:11,110 --> 00:05:12,830
China and Japan both sold.

125
00:05:13,170 --> 00:05:15,680
But Britain and Canada alone bought more than China

126
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:16,280
sold.

127
00:05:16,490 --> 00:05:19,730
One honest caveat: this data shows where bonds are

128
00:05:19,730 --> 00:05:22,290
held. Not always who really owns them.

129
00:05:22,710 --> 00:05:24,180
And the bank account that vanishes?

130
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:26,140
He's talking about a cyberattack.

131
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:28,320
That's a real security risk.

132
00:05:28,730 --> 00:05:30,410
But it's not proof the dollar is dying.

133
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:32,300
Verdict: too early to call.

134
00:05:32,730 --> 00:05:34,650
But up to the latest data, the money says

135
00:05:34,650 --> 00:05:35,460
the opposite.

136
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:38,000
Here's the picture, from our own numbers.

137
00:05:38,490 --> 00:05:40,580
Who lends to America, one year apart.

138
00:05:40,730 --> 00:05:42,990
Japan, down. China, down.

139
00:05:42,990 --> 00:05:45,230
Britain, up. Belgium, up.

140
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:47,650
The dollar system isn't collapsing.

141
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:49,160
It's changing hands.

142
00:05:49,620 --> 00:05:51,330
And that's the part his theory misses.

143
00:05:52,370 --> 00:05:54,270
Here's how it looks from our side of the

144
00:05:54,270 --> 00:05:54,870
world.

145
00:05:55,160 --> 00:05:57,450
In the Gulf, the dollar isn't a theory.

146
00:05:57,870 --> 00:05:59,690
It's built into the money itself.

147
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:02,240
The Saudi riyal has been tied to the dollar

148
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:07,340
since nineteen eighty-six. The Emirati dirham, officially since nineteen

149
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:10,320
ninety-seven, and in practice since nineteen eighty.

150
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:13,880
So if the dollar really collapsed, Gulf savings would

151
00:06:13,880 --> 00:06:16,980
sink with it. And yet those pegs have held

152
00:06:16,980 --> 00:06:18,400
through every crisis since.

153
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:20,920
Morocco does something in between.

154
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:24,550
Our dirham follows a basket: sixty percent euro, forty

155
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:25,310
percent dollar.

156
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:27,700
So for most of us, the real risk isn't

157
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:29,220
the dollar vanishing overnight.

158
00:06:29,590 --> 00:06:32,250
It's inflation, slowly eating what our savings can buy.

159
00:06:32,650 --> 00:06:35,360
And for your money, three simple rules.

160
00:06:35,690 --> 00:06:38,380
Don't sell everything because someone predicts the end of

161
00:06:38,380 --> 00:06:38,980
the world.

162
00:06:39,350 --> 00:06:41,700
Don't keep all your savings in one currency or

163
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:42,350
one asset.

164
00:06:42,790 --> 00:06:45,590
And be careful with anyone selling gold, crypto or

165
00:06:45,590 --> 00:06:47,750
courses as protection from the collapse.

166
00:06:48,430 --> 00:06:50,020
Fear is their business model.

167
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:52,580
That goes for anyone, on any channel.

168
00:06:52,990 --> 00:06:53,720
Including this one.

169
00:06:54,150 --> 00:06:57,310
This is education, not financial advice.

170
00:06:58,380 --> 00:07:00,760
One more thing. On the Diary of a CEO

171
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:02,370
podcast, he made a bet.

172
00:07:02,770 --> 00:07:03,850
A hundred dollars.

173
00:07:04,340 --> 00:07:07,740
He bet that on January twenty-first, twenty twenty-nine, Trump

174
00:07:07,740 --> 00:07:08,950
will still be in the White House.

175
00:07:09,340 --> 00:07:10,300
He gave two ways.

176
00:07:10,730 --> 00:07:14,290
A national emergency. Or Trump runs as vice president,

177
00:07:14,290 --> 00:07:15,520
and the president resigns.

178
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,360
He said nothing in the Constitution stops that.

179
00:07:18,790 --> 00:07:21,860
But no clause lets a president suspend the Constitution.

180
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:24,970
The twentieth amendment ends his term at noon on

181
00:07:24,970 --> 00:07:28,250
January twentieth. And the twelfth amendment says no one

182
00:07:28,250 --> 00:07:30,510
who can't be president can be vice president.

183
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:33,200
Many experts say that closes the door.

184
00:07:33,690 --> 00:07:36,240
Trump himself ruled out the vice president route last

185
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:38,020
year. He called it too cute.

186
00:07:38,410 --> 00:07:40,070
A few say there's still a gap.

187
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:43,240
So we're adding his bet to our Prediction Ledger.

188
00:07:43,950 --> 00:07:44,730
Entry number two.

189
00:07:45,190 --> 00:07:48,000
We'll score it on January twenty-first, twenty twenty-nine.

190
00:07:48,410 --> 00:07:50,300
Subscribe, and you'll see the result.

191
00:07:51,360 --> 00:07:53,410
So, is the dollar about to collapse?

192
00:07:53,820 --> 00:07:54,980
On the evidence, no.

193
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:56,800
Is it changing? Yes.

194
00:07:57,280 --> 00:07:59,880
Your turn. If he's right, what would you do

195
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:01,550
with your money? Tell me in the comments.

196
00:08:02,140 --> 00:08:04,270
And which prophet should we fact-check next?

197
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:06,590
Same world. Different lens.
